// Thinking Process:
// 1. High-Yield Concept: Chronic leukemias = neoplastic proliferations of MATURE lymphocytes. Four entities: CLL (CD5+CD20+ naive B cells with smudge cells; complications = hypogammaglobulinemia → infection death, AIHA, Richter transformation), Hairy cell (TRAP+, dry tap, RED pulp splenomegaly, NO lymphadenopathy, 2-CDA treatment), ATLL (HTLV-1, rash + lytic bone lesions + hypercalcemia + lymphadenopathy), and Mycosis fungoides/Sézary (CD4+ T-cells in skin with Pautrier microabscesses; cerebriform nuclei in blood).
// 2. The "Trap": Lytic bone lesions + hypercalcemia knee-jerks to multiple myeloma — but ATLL with rash mimics it. Splenomegaly knee-jerks to white pulp expansion — but hairy cell expands RED pulp. CD5 knee-jerks to T-cell — but CLL B-cells aberrantly express CD5.
// 3. Key Distractor: Multiple myeloma (when ATLL is correct) — the rash and HTLV-1 exposure are the discriminating features. CLL when hairy cell is correct.
// 4. Vignette Strategy: Include rash with lytic lesions to point at ATLL; include dry tap to point at hairy cell; include cerebriform nuclei to point at Sézary; include enlarging single node in a CLL patient to point at Richter.
//
